.TH "SERIALPORT.CLOSE" "3JS" "April 25, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
SerialPort.close \- SerialPort: close() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Dedicated Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]SerialPort.close()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that
resolves when the port closes.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
close()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]close()\f[R] closes the serial port if previously\-locked
\f[CR]SerialPort.readable\f[R] and \f[CR]SerialPort.writable\f[R]
members are unlocked, meaning the \f[CR]releaseLock()\f[R] methods have
been called for their respective reader and writer.
.PP
However, when continuously reading data from a serial device using a
loop, the associated readable stream will always be locked until the
reader encounters an error.
In this case, calling \f[CR]reader.cancel()\f[R] will force
\f[CR]reader.read()\f[R] to resolve immediately with
\f[CR]{ value: undefined, done: true }\f[R] allowing the loop to call
\f[CR]reader.releaseLock()\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Without transform streams.\f[R]

\f[B]let\f[R] keepReading = \f[B]true\f[R];
\f[B]let\f[R] reader;

\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] readUntilClosed() {
  \f[B]while\f[R] (port.readable && keepReading) {
    reader = port.readable.getReader();
    \f[B]try\f[R] {
      \f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R]) {
        \f[B]const\f[R] { value, done } = \f[B]await\f[R] reader.read();
        \f[B]if\f[R] (done) {
          \f[I]// reader.cancel() has been called.\f[R]
          \f[B]break\f[R];
        }
        \f[I]// value is a Uint8Array.\f[R]
        console.log(value);
      }
    } \f[B]catch\f[R] (error) {
      \f[I]// Handle error...\f[R]
    } \f[B]finally\f[R] {
      \f[I]// Allow the serial port to be closed later.\f[R]
      reader.releaseLock();
    }
  }

  \f[B]await\f[R] port.close();
}

\f[B]const\f[R] closedPromise = readUntilClosed();

document.querySelector(\(dqbutton\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[I]// User clicked a button to close the serial port.\f[R]
  keepReading = \f[B]false\f[R];
  \f[I]// Force reader.read() to resolve immediately and subsequently\f[R]
  \f[I]// call reader.releaseLock() in the loop example above.\f[R]
  reader.cancel();
  \f[B]await\f[R] closedPromise;
});
.EE
.PP
Closing a serial port is more complicated when using transform streams.
See \c
.UR https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/serial#close-port
Close a serial port
.UE \c
\ for guidance.
